{{Short description|Football tournament}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=June 2018}}
'''Group G of the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]]''' began on 15 June and ended on 25 June 2010.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Match Schedule 2010 FIFA World Cup South Africa |url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/64/42/24/fifawc2010matchschedulefinalversion.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091228213032/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/64/42/24/fifawc2010matchschedulefinalversion.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=28 December 2009 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |accessdate=6 December 2009 }}</ref> The group consisted of reigning [[Copa América]] champion [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]], [[North Korea national football team|North Korea]], the [[Ivory Coast national football team|Ivory Coast]] and [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]].

Brazil and Portugal were also drawn in the [[1966 FIFA World Cup#Group 3|same group]] at the [[1966 FIFA World Cup]], their previous official World Cup match. In 1966, the Portuguese team defeated Brazil 3–1, thus eliminating the [[1962 FIFA World Cup|defending champions]], to advance to the quarter-finals where they encountered and defeated North Korea (another team in Group G) 5–3 after trailing 0–3.

As winners of the group, Brazil advanced to play against [[Chile national football team|Chile]] – runners-up in [[2010 FIFA World Cup Group H|Group H]] – in the Round of 16, while Portugal – the Group G runners-up – met [[Spain national football team|Spain]], who won Group H. Ivory Coast finished third in the group and North Korea finished bottom. The North Koreans had a goal difference of &minus;11, the worst of any team at the tournament, largely due to the 7–0 defeat they suffered against Portugal; that match was Portugal's biggest-ever World Cup win and North Korea's heaviest-ever defeat. It was also the first time a live football match was broadcast on North Korean television.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/21/north-korea-live-match-tv-7-0-beating|title=North Korea broadcasts live match on TV for first time (pity they lost 7-0)|publisher=[[The Guardian]]|date=21 June 2010|accessdate=24 September 2010|first=Jonathan|last=Watts|first2=David|last2=Hytner}}</ref>

